Discohash (posted here) is the fastest one I made, it's simple and doesn't rely on any arch-specific optimizations or vector instructions (AVX etc ~ tho I suppose...they could be added? I'm definitely no expert in them, I barely get away with doing the C/C++ implementations!)
The main mixing round function is:
  mix(const int A) 
{
      const int B = A + 1;

      ds[A] *= P;
      ds[A] = rot(ds[A], 23);
      ds[A] *= Q;

      ds[B] ^= ds[A];

      ds[B] *= P;
      ds[B] = rot(ds[B], 23);
      ds[B] *= Q;
  }

where P and Q are two randomly chosen 64-bit primes (P is actually the largest unsigned 64-bit prime!), and A is an index into the 256-bit internal state.
I love it because of its simplicity (and because I Created it :) ha), but I think it has some room to grow. An interesting design feature is the internal state is actually variable, it can be larger than 32-bytes.
Some future directions (for anyone who is interested and wants to extend it):
- add a sponge construction to create an infinite output stream / keystream generator
- add AVX instructions (or other optimizations to squeeze more out of a serial implementation)
- figure out how to use a Merkle construction on discohash (like the Blake hashes^4 use to improve their not-that-impressive serial speed using parallelization), hard for discohash I think because state operations are essentially "chained together"
- see if it can be parameterized with other pairs of primes (perhaps into a "universal" family of functions). P & Q in discohash have the interesting and quite rare property that P - Q = P ^ Q (their difference equals their XOR), I surmise that may required for other P and Q to work in discohash.
I just added a PR to Smhasher^5 to update the hash to a faster version and fix some implementation issues related to memory alignment that after much fuffing about with ChatGPT I was thankfully able to resolve! Relief!
I'm no math expert, nor an expert in optimization, nor cryptanalysis, I basically just developed these hashes intuitively, flying by the seat of my pants! But they worked, hoo rah! :)
